16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"January–May 1864","place":"Washington, Virginia, Tennessee, Mississippi, and north Georgia","result":"Grant prepared coordinated offensives against the main Confederate armies while Sherman repaired his supply system and secured the approaches to north Georgia before beginning the Atlanta campaign","facts":[{"label":"U.S. soldiers mustered by 1864","value":"Nearly 2 million"},{"label":"U.S. military deaths by 1864","value":"At least 250,000"},{"label":"Railroad miles supporting Chattanooga","value":"292 miles"},{"label":"Locomotives initially available","value":"39"},{"label":"Railcars initially available","value":"About 400"},{"label":"McCallum’s supply goal","value":"200 locomotives and 3,000 cars"},{"label":"Longstreet’s force near Knoxville","value":"About 20,000 men"}],"maps":[{"title":"The Atlanta campaign area","caption":"Sherman’s armies would advance from Chattanooga through Dalton and the mountain passes of north Georgia toward Atlanta. The Western and Atlantic Railroad was their principal supply line.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008628278/","image":"/images/atlanta-campaign-map-1.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Atlanta campaign from Chattanooga through north Georgia"},{"title":"The Chattanooga–Atlanta corridor today","caption":"Chattanooga was the U.S. base for the spring campaign. Dalton guarded the railroad and the direct route south toward Atlanta.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-85.65%2C33.55%2C-84.05%2C35.35&layer=mapnik&marker=35.0456%2C-85.3097"},{"title":"The railroad to Atlanta","caption":"The Western and Atlantic Railroad connected Chattanooga, Dalton, and Atlanta. Sherman first had to create enough rail capacity to supply several armies over this route.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g3921p.rr006140/","image":"/images/western-atlantic-birds-eye-map.jpg","alt":"Birds-eye map of the Western and Atlantic Railroad between Chattanooga and Atlanta"},{"title":"Sherman’s Meridian expedition","caption":"Before turning toward Atlanta, Sherman moved east from Vicksburg to Meridian to destroy railroads and reduce the threat to the Mississippi River and his western flank.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-91.10%2C31.70%2C-88.45%2C33.10&layer=mapnik&marker=32.3643%2C-88.7037"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:19","audioTime":79,"title":"The podcast begins the Atlanta campaign","detail":"The campaign is introduced as a military operation whose outcome also affected Northern support for the war and Lincoln’s reelection."},{"time":"2:28","audioTime":148,"title":"Grant planned simultaneous offensives","detail":"Grant intended to pressure the principal Confederate armies at the same time so they could not send reinforcements from one theater to another."},{"time":"3:02","audioTime":182,"title":"Sherman received the Atlanta assignment","detail":"Sherman’s armies were to move from Chattanooga against Joseph E. Johnston’s Army of Tennessee and into the Confederate interior."},{"time":"4:53","audioTime":293,"title":"The spring campaign dates are established","detail":"Sherman entered north Georgia on May 5 and opened operations against Johnston on May 7, as Grant began the Overland Campaign in Virginia."},{"time":"6:12","audioTime":372,"title":"The account returns to January 1864","detail":"The episode explains the political, military, and logistical conditions that shaped the spring offensives."},{"time":"6:41","audioTime":401,"title":"The United States held major 1863 victories","detail":"Gettysburg, Vicksburg, Port Hudson, and Missionary Ridge improved the military position, but the war remained unfinished."},{"time":"7:52","audioTime":472,"title":"U.S. losses were already extensive","detail":"Nearly two million men had been mustered and at least 250,000 had died; disease caused most noncombat deaths."},{"time":"9:01","audioTime":541,"title":"War weariness affected Northern politics","detail":"Casualties, taxation, conscription, restrictions on civil liberties, and opposition to emancipation increased criticism of the administration."},{"time":"10:01","audioTime":601,"title":"Democrats opposed Republican war policy","detail":"The party’s platform emphasized restoring the prewar Union and Constitution and included substantial opposition to emancipation."},{"time":"14:09","audioTime":849,"title":"Recent elections still favored Republicans","detail":"At the start of 1864 Democrats held the governorships of New York and New Jersey, while Republicans had won the other state elections held in autumn 1863."},{"time":"17:23","audioTime":1043,"title":"Battlefield results determined political support","detail":"Military failure and rising casualties could reduce support for Lincoln, while successful campaigns could strengthen it before the November election."},{"time":"21:36","audioTime":1296,"title":"Lincoln needed an effective senior commander","detail":"He sought a general who would use U.S. manpower and industry, coordinate military operations with national policy, and produce results before public support collapsed."},{"time":"21:52","audioTime":1312,"title":"Grant became the leading candidate","detail":"His record at Fort Donelson, Vicksburg, and Chattanooga convinced Lincoln that he might direct the armies effectively."},{"time":"23:02","audioTime":1382,"title":"A winter advance was impractical","detail":"Bad roads, exhausted troops, inadequate rations, and an incomplete transportation system prevented an immediate move into Georgia."},{"time":"23:37","audioTime":1417,"title":"Supply was the central problem","detail":"Warehouses at Louisville, Nashville, and Memphis held supplies, but the armies at Chattanooga and Knoxville remained on reduced rations because the railroad could not move enough freight."},{"time":"24:33","audioTime":1473,"title":"Daniel McCallum rebuilt railroad capacity","detail":"Grant sent the military railroad director to reopen and improve the route to Chattanooga. The first train carrying crackers arrived there in mid-January."},{"time":"25:23","audioTime":1523,"title":"The rail system lacked equipment","detail":"Only 39 locomotives and about 400 cars served 292 miles in Tennessee, compared with 40 locomotives and 800 cars serving about 70 miles for the Army of the Potomac."},{"time":"26:01","audioTime":1561,"title":"Grant centralized railroad control","detail":"McCallum received authority over the railroads in the Military Division of the Mississippi and set a goal of 200 locomotives and 3,000 cars."},{"time":"26:30","audioTime":1590,"title":"Longstreet remained near Knoxville","detail":"About 20,000 Confederate troops still occupied eastern Tennessee. Grant had to determine whether they could threaten Chattanooga or reinforce Johnston."},{"time":"27:39","audioTime":1659,"title":"Foster’s advance ended without a battle","detail":"Grant ordered John G. Foster to drive Longstreet away. Foster moved with about 20,000 men but turned back after finding the Confederate position too strong."},{"time":"28:04","audioTime":1684,"title":"Schofield replaced Foster","detail":"Grant placed John M. Schofield in command of the Army of the Ohio and expected him to secure eastern Tennessee before joining the Atlanta operation."},{"time":"29:05","audioTime":1745,"title":"Sherman prepared a strike into Mississippi","detail":"Grant authorized Sherman to move from Vicksburg to Meridian and destroy railroads, supplies, and other military infrastructure before the main spring campaign."},{"time":"30:04","audioTime":1804,"title":"The Meridian expedition began","detail":"After warning Mississippi residents on January 31, Sherman marched east from Vicksburg on February 3."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Abraham Lincoln","command":"President and commander-in-chief","subordinates":"Set national war policy and sought a general able to coordinate all theaters"},{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Military Division of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Directed western operations and developed the coordinated strategy; promoted to general-in-chief in March 1864"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Led the Meridian expedition and later received command of Grant’s western military division"},{"commander":"George H. Thomas","command":"Army of the Cumberland","subordinates":"Held Chattanooga and prepared the largest army for the advance into Georgia"},{"commander":"John G. Foster","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Tried to move against Longstreet in eastern Tennessee"},{"commander":"John M. Schofield","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Replaced Foster and prepared to join Sherman’s spring campaign"},{"commander":"Daniel C. McCallum","command":"U.S. Military Railroads","subordinates":"Repaired, expanded, and operated the rail network supplying the western armies"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Jefferson Davis","command":"Confederate president and commander-in-chief","subordinates":"Directed Confederate military policy"},{"commander":"Joseph E. Johnston","command":"Army of Tennessee","subordinates":"Defended north Georgia from his base at Dalton"},{"commander":"James Longstreet","command":"Detached First Corps, Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Remained in eastern Tennessee with about 20,000 men"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"Linked military success to preservation of public support for the war and prepared for the 1864 election","side":"United States","image":"/images/abraham-lincoln-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008680391/"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Planned coordinated offensives and directed the logistical preparation of the western armies"},{"name":"William T. Sherman","role":"Led the Meridian expedition and prepared to command the advance toward Atlanta","side":"United States","image":"/images/william-t-sherman.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666274/"},{"name":"Daniel C. McCallum","role":"Expanded the military railroad system required to supply the Atlanta campaign","side":"United States"},{"name":"George H. Thomas","role":"Commanded the Army of the Cumberland at Chattanooga","side":"United States"},{"name":"John G. Foster","role":"Attempted to drive Longstreet from eastern Tennessee before being replaced","side":"United States"},{"name":"John M. Schofield","role":"Replaced Foster and later brought the Army of the Ohio into Sherman’s campaign","side":"United States"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Commanded the Confederate force near Knoxville that threatened the U.S. flank","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Joseph E. Johnston","role":"Commanded the Confederate army defending the approach to Atlanta","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/joseph-e-johnston.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666539/"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/abraham-lincoln-1864.jpg","alt":"Abraham Lincoln seated in January 1864","caption":"Lincoln needed battlefield progress to sustain support for the war and his administration.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008680391/"},{"image":"/images/ulysses-s-grant.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Ulysses S. Grant","caption":"Grant planned simultaneous pressure against the main Confederate armies.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666428/"},{"image":"/images/william-t-sherman.jpg","alt":"Portrait of William T. Sherman","caption":"Sherman secured the western flank in Mississippi before beginning the Atlanta campaign.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666274/"},{"image":"/images/western-atlantic-birds-eye-map.jpg","alt":"Birds-eye map of the Western and Atlantic Railroad","caption":"The railroad from Chattanooga to Atlanta became Sherman’s main supply route.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g3921p.rr006140/"},{"image":"/images/atlanta-railroad-depot.jpg","alt":"Railroad depot and engine house in Atlanta after its capture","caption":"Atlanta’s rail connections made the city a strategic objective of the campaign.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666635/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 502 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/08/24/civil-war-podcast-episode-502/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Atlanta and Savannah Campaigns, 1864","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-13.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army logistics anthology","url":"https://history.army.mil/Portals/143/Images/Publications/Publication%20By%20Title%20Images/U%20Pdf/cmhPub_68-1.pdf"},{"label":"National Park Service: Grant’s Path to Victory","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/ulysses-s-grant-s-path-to-victory-the-1864-overland-campaign.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Atlanta Campaign order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/atlanta-campaign-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress Atlanta campaign map series","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008628278/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Western and Atlantic Railroad map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/g3921p.rr006140/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Abraham Lincoln portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008680391/"}]}}]}]
